export const K6={title:'SALT mass casualty triage (Skill I)',hook:'Triage comes from the French word “trier,” to sort. When there are more patients than rescuers, sorting them quickly—so the people who will die without immediate help get it first—saves the most lives. SALT is the national standard for mass casualty triage, and Skill I tests it on two or three patients in four minutes.',objectives:['Explain the purpose of mass casualty triage and the SALT acronym.','Conduct a global sort using voice commands.','Perform individual assessments and lifesaving interventions.','Assign SALT categories and tag colors correctly.'],sections:[
['What SALT means',[
'**SALT** stands for **Sort, Assess, Lifesaving interventions, Treatment and/or Transport**. It was developed by national experts as a standard triage method for all ages and all types of incidents. Rescuers first **sort** patients by their ability to move, **assess** each one individually, perform quick **lifesaving interventions** and then assign a category that determines the order of **treatment and transport**.',
'Triage is **dynamic**: patients are reassessed because conditions change. A patient tagged Delayed can become Immediate. CERT teams use triage in the field and again when patients reach treatment areas.',
"Triage is needed whenever the number of patients exceeds the resources available—after building collapses, explosions, transportation crashes, earthquakes or severe storms. The first rescuers on scene set the pace for the whole response, so a fast, organized sort saves many lives.",
order('SALT',['Sort (global sort)','Assess individually','Lifesaving interventions','Treatment and/or transport'])]],
['The global sort: Skill I steps 1–5',[
'**1.** Don PPE (helmet, eye protection, work gloves, mask and sturdy shoes; helmet, boots and gloves may be verbalized). **2.** Size up the scene. **3.** Call out loudly: “**Everyone who can hear me, please move to [a designated safe area], and we will help you.**” Those who walk are assessed **last**. **4.** Call out: “**If you need help, wave your arm or move your leg, and we will be there to help you in a few minutes.**” Those who wave or move purposefully are assessed **second**.',
'**5.** Move quickly to those who are **still** or have **obvious life threats**—they are assessed **first**. The global sort takes seconds and directs rescuers to the most critical patients. In the event, the scenario may allow the team to split up to assess patients faster.',
order('Global sort priority',['Still or obvious life threat—assess first','Waves or moves purposefully—assess second','Walks to the safe area—assess last'])]],
['Lifesaving interventions',[
'During the individual assessment, perform quick **lifesaving interventions** that require little time and equipment: **control major hemorrhage** (pressure or tourniquet), **open the airway** (and for a child, give **two rescue breaths** if not breathing), **chest decompression** (professionals only) and **auto-injector antidotes** for certain chemical exposures (professionals or trained personnel). Do these only if they are within your training and equipment.',
'If a patient is **not breathing** after one attempt to open the airway (and, for a child, two rescue breaths), the patient is categorized as **Dead** (black or black-and-white striped). This feels hard, but continuing would take a rescuer away from patients who can still be saved.',
flip('Lifesaving interventions',[['Major hemorrhage','Pressure or tourniquet'],['Airway','Open it; two breaths for a child'],['Chest decompression','Professionals only'],['Antidote auto-injector','Trained personnel for chemical exposures'],['Not breathing after airway opened','Dead (black)'],['Interventions','Quick, within training and equipment']])]],
['The individual assessment and categories: Skill I steps 6–7',[
'For each patient, ask four questions: **Has a peripheral pulse? Breathing normally (not in respiratory distress)? Major hemorrhage controlled? Follows commands or makes purposeful movements?** If the answer to **any** is **no**, the patient meets **Immediate** criteria; then ask, “**Is the patient likely to survive given available resources?**” If **yes**, tag **Immediate (red)**; if **no**, tag **Expectant (gray)**.',
'If the answer to **all four** questions is **yes**, ask whether the injuries are **minor**. If the injuries are **not minor and require care**, tag **Delayed (yellow)**—care can wait without increasing death or disability. If the injuries are **minor and need little or no care**, tag **Minimal (green)**. A patient who is not breathing after the airway is opened is **Dead (black)**. **7.** Assess and tag each patient correctly; **8–9.** both rescuers communicate throughout.',
{t:'table',caption:'SALT categories',head:['Category','Color','Criteria'],rows:[['Immediate','Red','Fails any of the four checks but likely to survive with available resources'],['Delayed','Yellow','Passes all four checks; injuries not minor and need care'],['Minimal','Green','Passes all four checks; minor injuries'],['Expectant','Gray','Fails a check and unlikely to survive with available resources'],['Dead','Black','Not breathing after the airway is opened (two breaths for a child)']]},
order('Individual assessment',['Peripheral pulse?','Breathing normally?','Major hemorrhage controlled?','Follows commands or purposeful movement?','Any “no”: likely to survive? Immediate (red) or Expectant (gray)','All “yes”: minor injuries? Minimal (green) or Delayed (yellow)'])]],
['Practice triage',[
'Practice with quick verbal summaries: “Patient A: no radial pulse, breathing fast, follows commands—**Immediate**, red.” Mark each patient with tape or a tag clearly so others do not reassess unnecessarily. Write simple notes: category, location and time. Move fast—about **30–60 seconds per patient** in a real incident.',
sort('Assign the SALT category',['Immediate (red)','Delayed (yellow)','Minimal (green)','Expectant (gray)','Dead (black)'],[['Pulse, normal breathing, follows commands, open leg fracture',1],['Walking with small scrapes',2],['No breathing after the airway is opened',4],['Bleeding controlled, confused and does not follow commands, likely to survive',0],['Massive head injury, not expected to survive with available resources',3],['Breathing in distress but likely to survive',0]]),
kase('Triage scenario',{a:{text:'Patient B has a radial pulse, breathes normally, has no major bleeding and follows commands, but has a deformed lower leg. (Scenario invented for practice.) What is the category?',choices:[['Delayed (yellow)','b','Correct. Serious injury, but care can wait.',true],['Immediate (red)','b','All four checks are passed.',false]]},b:{text:'Patient C is not breathing. You open the airway and there is still no breathing. What is the category?',choices:[['Dead (black)','c','Correct. Move on to the next patient.',true],['Immediate (red)','c','In SALT, not breathing after the airway is opened is Dead.',false]]},c:{text:'You tag both patients, tell your partner and move to the next patient.',end:true}})]],
["Triage in the real world",["Real mass-casualty incidents are noisy and frightening. Rescuers should **move quickly** (about 30–60 seconds per patient), resist the urge to stop and treat everyone fully and remember that triage is about **sorting**, not complete care. Use simple tags—tape on the shoulder or a card tied to the wrist—with the category, time and a short note. Walking patients in the safe area are still reassessed, because some will worsen.","Triage decisions can be emotionally hard, especially tagging patients as Expectant or Dead. These decisions are based on **available resources**: when more help arrives, Expectant patients may receive care. Rescuers should debrief after the event and support each other. Other triage systems exist (such as START), but HOSA’s CERT event uses **SALT**, so learn its questions and colors exactly.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Good triage practice?","buckets":["Good practice","Poor practice"],"items":[["Spending 30–60 seconds per patient",0],["Fully bandaging every minor wound before moving on",1],["Reassessing patients as conditions change",0],["Using clear tags with category and time",0],["Skipping the global sort",1],["Using a different color system than SALT in the event",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['SALT: Sort, Assess, Lifesaving interventions, Treatment/Transport; triage is dynamic.','Global sort: “Move to [safe area]” (walkers assessed last); “Wave your arm or move your leg” (assessed second); still or obvious life threats first.','Four checks—peripheral pulse, normal breathing, hemorrhage controlled, follows commands: any “no” → Immediate (red) if likely to survive, Expectant (gray) if not; all “yes” → Delayed (yellow) or Minimal (green); not breathing after airway opened → Dead (black).'],terms:[['Triage','Sorting patients by the urgency of their needs.'],['Global sort','Using voice commands to sort patients by their ability to move.'],['Expectant','Patients unlikely to survive with available resources (gray).']],mcqs:[q('The rescuer team has determined that a patient is triaged as delayed. What color tag would be used?','Yellow','Red','Green','Gray','HOSA sample question.'),q('What does the S in SALT stand for first?','Sort','Save','Splint','Search','Then Assess.'),q('In the global sort, who is assessed first?','Those who are still or have obvious life threats','Those who walk','Those who wave','The loudest','Most critical.'),q('A patient who fails any of the four checks and is likely to survive is:','Immediate (red)','Delayed (yellow)','Minimal (green)','Expectant (gray)','Needs care now.'),q('Gray tags indicate:','Expectant','Dead','Minimal','Delayed','Unlikely to survive with available resources.'),q('A child not breathing after the airway is opened should receive:','Two rescue breaths','Ten compressions','A tourniquet','Nothing','Then categorize.'),q('Which is one of the four individual assessment questions?','Follows commands or makes purposeful movements?','What is your name?','Do you have insurance?','Is your house damaged?','Mental status check.'),q('A walking patient with minor scrapes is:','Minimal (green)','Immediate (red)','Delayed (yellow)','Dead (black)','Minor injuries.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how a CERT team performs SALT triage on three patients, including the global sort, the individual assessment questions and how each SALT category is assigned.',rubric:[['Preparation and global sort',['ppe','size-up','safe area','walk','wave','move your leg','still']],['Lifesaving interventions',['hemorrhage','airway','two rescue breaths','child','tourniquet']],['Four questions',['peripheral pulse','breathing','hemorrhage','commands','purposeful']],['Categories',['immediate','red','delayed','yellow','minimal','green','expectant','gray','dead','black','likely to survive']]],model:'After donning PPE and sizing up the scene, we perform a global sort. One of us calls out loudly, “Everyone who can hear me, please move to the parking lot and we will help you”; those who walk are assessed last. Then we call, “If you need help, wave your arm or move your leg and we will be there in a few minutes”; those who move purposefully are assessed second. We go first to patients who are still or have obvious life threats. For each patient, we perform quick lifesaving interventions within our training, such as controlling major bleeding with pressure or a tourniquet and opening the airway; if a child is not breathing, we give two rescue breaths. A patient who still is not breathing after the airway is opened is tagged Dead, black. For everyone else, we ask four questions: Does the patient have a peripheral pulse? Are they breathing normally? Is major hemorrhage controlled? Do they follow commands or make purposeful movements? If the answer to any is no, the patient meets Immediate criteria; we then ask whether they are likely to survive with the resources available—if yes, they are Immediate, red; if no, Expectant, gray. If all four answers are yes and the injuries are not minor and need care, the patient is Delayed, yellow; if the injuries are minor, the patient is Minimal, green. We tag each patient clearly, communicate with each other and reassess as conditions change.'},sources:[RULES,SALT,CHEMM_SALT,IS317]};

export const K7={title:'Medical operations I: head-to-toe assessment and treatment areas (Skill III)',hook:'Once the life-threatening problems are handled, CERT members look for every other injury—carefully, from head to toe, without moving the spine. A hidden pelvic fracture or a puncture wound under clothing can turn into a crisis later. Skill III tests the head-to-toe assessment, and medical operations make up 20% of the test.',objectives:['Organize disaster medical operations and treatment areas.','Conduct a head-to-toe assessment using DCAP-BTLS following HOSA Skill III.','Check pulse, movement and sensation and protect the spine.','Document injuries on the CERT Victim Treatment Area Record.'],sections:[
['Disaster medical operations',[
'CERT medical operations include **triage**, **treatment** and **documentation**. Treatment areas are set up in a **safe location** close to the incident but away from hazards, with clear entry and exit routes, room for expansion and access for vehicles. Areas are labeled **I (Immediate)**, **D (Delayed)** and **M (Minimal)**, plus a separate, discreet **morgue** area for the dead. Patients are reassessed as they arrive.',
"A **morgue** area is placed out of view of survivors, and the dead are treated with respect and documented with their location and any identifying information. Personal belongings stay with patients, labeled when possible, so families can be reunited with them later.",
'In the treatment area, rescuers perform **head-to-toe assessments**, treat injuries, monitor patients, keep them warm and document everything. Sanitation and hygiene—hand hygiene, glove changes between patients, safe disposal of waste and clean water—prevent the spread of disease.',
match('Treatment areas',[['I','Immediate patients'],['D','Delayed patients'],['M','Minimal patients'],['Morgue','Separate area for the dead'],['Location','Safe, close and accessible to vehicles'],['Reassessment','Done as patients arrive and over time']])]],
['Starting the assessment: Skill III steps 1–5',[
'Skill III is done by **each rescuer on a separate live patient** in a structure with light damage (5 minutes). **1.** Don PPE. **2.** Identify a patient. **3.** If the patient is responsive and able to talk, ask, “**How were you hurt?**” **4.** If conscious, **ask permission** to conduct the assessment. **5.** If responsive, ask about **injuries, pain, bleeding or other symptoms**, and check for **medical ID** emblems on a bracelet or necklace.',
'The mechanism of injury—a fall, crushing, a blast—helps predict hidden injuries. Patients with a head or neck injury may have spinal damage, so keep the head and spine as still as possible throughout. Talking to the patient throughout reduces anxiety and helps monitor mental status.',
order('Before the head-to-toe check',['Don PPE','Identify the patient','Ask “How were you hurt?”','Ask permission if conscious','Ask about injuries, pain, bleeding and symptoms','Check for medical ID jewelry'])]],
['DCAP-BTLS and the head-to-toe order: Skill III step 6',[
'Look and feel for **DCAP-BTLS**: **D**eformities, **C**ontusions, **A**brasions, **P**unctures or penetrations, **B**urns, **T**enderness, **L**acerations and **S**welling. Check body parts **in this order**: **a.** head, **b.** neck, **c.** shoulders, **d.** chest, **e.** arms, **f.** abdomen, **g.** pelvis and **h.** legs, verbalizing what you find at each area. **i.** Follow DCAP-BTLS in that order. **j.** Check **pulse, movement and sensation (PMS)** in the arms and legs.',
'Use gentle pressure and compare both sides. Never move the head or neck to look under it—slide your hands. For the pelvis, press gently inward and downward only once; pain or instability suggests a fracture and possible internal bleeding.',
order('Head-to-toe order',['Head','Neck','Shoulders','Chest','Arms','Abdomen','Pelvis','Legs']),
flip('DCAP-BTLS',[['D','Deformities'],['C','Contusions'],['A','Abrasions'],['P','Punctures or penetrations'],['B','Burns'],['T','Tenderness'],['L','Lacerations'],['S','Swelling']])]],
['Finishing the assessment: Skill III steps 7–14',[
'**7.** **Check your own gloved hands for blood** throughout the assessment—blood on your gloves can reveal hidden bleeding under clothing or behind the patient. **8.** Verbalize your observations. **9.** Talk to the patient throughout to reduce anxiety. **10.** **Minimize movement of the head and spine.** **11.** Tell the judge the **type of treatment** needed (for example, splinting, bandaging or treatment for shock).',
'**12.** **Document** injuries correctly on the **CERT Victim Treatment Area Record**—name or description, triage category, injuries found, treatment given and time. **13.** Use alcohol-based hand rub. **14.** Communicate with the patient, partner and others. Documentation lets professionals pick up care without repeating the whole assessment.',
"Reassess patients periodically: injuries may swell, bleeding may restart and shock may develop. Any change in breathing, color, mental status or bleeding is reported to the treatment area leader, and the patient may be moved to a higher-priority area.",
order('Finishing Skill III',['Check your gloves for blood throughout','Verbalize findings','Reassure the patient','Minimize head and spine movement','State the needed treatment','Document on the Victim Treatment Area Record','Hand hygiene'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Head-to-toe findings',{a:{text:'While checking the chest, you notice blood on your gloves, but there is no obvious wound in front. (Scenario invented for practice.) What does this suggest?',choices:[['Hidden bleeding, possibly on the back or under clothing—look carefully without moving the spine','b','Correct. Checking your gloves finds hidden injuries.',true],['Your gloves are dirty from earlier—ignore it','b','Blood on gloves must be investigated.',false]]},b:{text:'When you press the pelvis gently, the patient cries out in pain. What do you report?',choices:[['Possible pelvic fracture and risk of internal bleeding—needs priority transport','c','Correct. Pelvic injuries can bleed heavily.',true],['Normal finding','c','Pelvic pain after trauma is significant.',false]]},c:{text:'You document the findings, treat for shock and request that the patient be moved to the Immediate area.',end:true}}),
chk('What does PMS stand for in an extremity check?',['Pulse, movement and sensation','Pain, mobility and swelling','Pressure, muscle and skin','Pupils, mouth and speech'],'Check before and after splinting too.')]],
["Working in the treatment area",["In the treatment area, each patient receives a full **head-to-toe assessment**, treatment for injuries found, ongoing monitoring of breathing, circulation and mental status, and protection from cold, sun and rain. Patients are reassessed regularly and moved between areas if their condition changes. Rescuers keep supplies organized, change gloves between patients and dispose of waste in marked containers.","Documentation tracks every patient: identification or description, injuries, treatment, triage category, times and where the patient goes when transported. A **treatment area leader** assigns rescuers, tracks supplies and communicates with the command post. When EMS arrives, rescuers give a brief report—category, main injuries, treatments and time of tourniquet application—and hand over records.",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"Treatment area workflow","steps":["Receive and reassess the patient","Place in the I, D or M area","Perform a head-to-toe assessment","Treat injuries and monitor","Document on the treatment record","Report to EMS and transfer records"]}]],
["Special considerations during assessment",["Adapt the assessment to the patient. **Children** may be frightened—kneel to their level, explain simply and keep caregivers nearby. **Older adults** may have medical conditions, hearing loss or fragile skin and bones. People with **disabilities** may use service animals, mobility devices or communication aids—ask how best to help. Patients who do not speak your language may need gestures, pictures or interpreters.","Respect **privacy** and **dignity**: expose only the area you are checking and cover the patient afterward. Some patients may have injuries hidden by clothing, so cut clothing if needed rather than moving the spine. Note medications, allergies and medical conditions from ID jewelry or the patient so responders can continue care.",{"t":"check","q":"How should you check an area hidden by clothing on a patient with a possible spine injury?","options":["Cut the clothing rather than move the spine","Sit the patient up","Roll the patient quickly","Skip that area"],"why":"Protect the spine while finding hidden injuries."}]]
],takeaways:['Medical operations: triage, treatment and documentation; treatment areas I, D and M plus a separate morgue in a safe, accessible location.','Skill III: PPE, ask how they were hurt, permission, injuries and medical ID; DCAP-BTLS (deformities, contusions, abrasions, punctures, burns, tenderness, lacerations, swelling) in order: head, neck, shoulders, chest, arms, abdomen, pelvis, legs; check PMS.','Check your gloves for blood, minimize head and spine movement, talk to the patient, state the needed treatment and document on the Victim Treatment Area Record.'],terms:[['DCAP-BTLS','Deformities, contusions, abrasions, punctures, burns, tenderness, lacerations and swelling.'],['PMS','Pulse, movement and sensation in an extremity.'],['Treatment area','A designated space where triaged patients receive care.']],mcqs:[q('What does the P in DCAP-BTLS stand for?','Punctures or penetrations','Pulse','Pain','Pelvis','Deformities, contusions, abrasions, punctures…'),q('Which body area is checked immediately after the shoulders?','Chest','Legs','Pelvis','Head','Order: head, neck, shoulders, chest…'),q('Why check your gloves for blood during the assessment?','To find hidden bleeding','To keep them clean','To decide when to leave','It is not needed','Blood reveals hidden wounds.'),q('What is the first question to a responsive patient in Skill III?','“How were you hurt?”','“What is your insurance?”','“Can you walk?”','“Where do you live?”','Mechanism of injury.'),q('Treatment area “D” is for:','Delayed patients','Dead patients','Doctors','Documentation','The morgue is separate.'),q('What should you minimize during the assessment?','Movement of the head and spine','Talking','Documentation','Hand hygiene','Possible spinal injury.'),q('Injuries are documented on the:','CERT Victim Treatment Area Record','Back of the patient’s hand','Judge’s sheet only','Nothing','Standard form.'),q('Which is the last area checked in the head-to-toe sequence?','Legs','Head','Abdomen','Arms','Ends with the legs.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how to perform a head-to-toe assessment following HOSA Skill III, including DCAP-BTLS, the order of body areas and how findings are documented.',rubric:[['Opening',['ppe','how were you hurt','permission','injuries','pain','medical id']],['DCAP-BTLS',['deformities','contusions','abrasions','punctures','burns','tenderness','lacerations','swelling']],['Order and checks',['head','neck','shoulders','chest','arms','abdomen','pelvis','legs','pulse','movement','sensation']],['Safety and documentation',['gloves','blood','spine','talk','treatment','victim treatment area record','hand rub']]],model:'After putting on PPE, I identify my patient and, if they can talk, ask, “How were you hurt?” and ask permission to assess them. I ask about injuries, pain, bleeding and other symptoms and look for a medical ID bracelet or necklace. Then I check the body from top to bottom, looking and feeling for DCAP-BTLS—deformities, contusions, abrasions, punctures, burns, tenderness, lacerations and swelling—in this order: head, neck, shoulders, chest, arms, abdomen, pelvis and legs, verbalizing my observations at each area. I check pulse, movement and sensation in the arms and legs. Throughout, I check my own gloves for blood, which can reveal hidden bleeding under clothing or behind the patient, and I minimize movement of the head and spine by sliding my hands rather than moving the patient. I keep talking to the patient to reduce anxiety and monitor their mental status. When I finish, I tell the judge what treatment is needed—for example, splinting a deformed forearm and treating for shock—and document the injuries, triage category, treatment and time on the CERT Victim Treatment Area Record so professional responders can continue care. I finish with alcohol-based hand rub and clear communication with my partner.'},sources:[RULES,IS317,READY_CERT]};

export const K8={title:'Medical operations II: burns, wounds, fractures and splinting (Skill V)',hook:'In a disaster, many patients will have burns, cuts and broken bones that are not immediately life-threatening but still need care—often for hours before professionals arrive. Good splinting reduces pain and prevents further damage, and clean water and sanitation prevent the infections that follow disasters. Skill V tests splinting a closed fracture as a team.',objectives:['Classify burns and provide burn first aid.','Clean and dress wounds and recognize when to seek help.','Recognize fractures, dislocations, sprains and strains and splint a closed fracture following HOSA Skill V.','Apply sanitation and public health measures after disasters.'],sections:[
['Burns',[
'**Superficial (first-degree)** burns affect the outer skin layer: **reddened, dry skin, pain and swelling** (like sunburn). **Partial-thickness (second-degree)** burns extend into the dermis: **blisters**, wet or shiny skin, severe pain and swelling. **Full-thickness (third-degree)** burns destroy the skin layers and may involve deeper tissue: **white, brown or charred**, leathery skin that may be **painless** because nerves are destroyed.',
'First aid: **stop the burning**—remove the person from the source and remove smoldering clothing and jewelry unless stuck to the skin. **Cool** the burn with cool, clean water for several minutes (not ice); cover loosely with dry, sterile dressings; do not break blisters or apply ointments, butter or home remedies; keep the patient warm to prevent hypothermia; and treat for shock. Burns to the face, hands, feet, genitals, airway or large areas need professional care. Smoke and **airway burns** (singed nose hairs, soot around the mouth, hoarseness) are emergencies.',
sort('Burn classification',['Superficial','Partial thickness','Full thickness'],[['Reddened, dry and painful skin',0],['Blisters and shiny, wet skin',1],['Charred, leathery skin with little pain',2],['Sunburn-like swelling',0],['Severe pain with blisters',1],['White or brown skin that feels leathery',2]])]],
['Wounds and other conditions',[
'For wounds, control bleeding first, then **clean** the wound by **irrigating** with clean water, cover with a sterile dressing and secure with a bandage that is snug but not too tight. **Dressings** go directly on the wound; **bandages** hold dressings in place. Watch for infection: redness, swelling, warmth, pus and red streaks. Impaled objects are stabilized in place, not removed. Amputated parts are wrapped in clean gauze, placed in a plastic bag and kept cool (not directly on ice) to travel with the patient.',
'Other disaster conditions include **hypothermia** (shivering, confusion—warm gradually), **heat illness** (move to shade, cool, hydrate), **crush injuries**, **nosebleeds** (lean forward and pinch the soft part of the nose), **chest injuries** and **allergic reactions**. Patients with chronic conditions may run out of medications; note this for responders.',
"Use clean or sterile supplies whenever possible and improvise carefully when they run out: clean cloth can serve as a dressing, and newspapers, boards or pillows can become splints.",
match('Wound care terms',[['Dressing','Covers the wound directly'],['Bandage','Holds the dressing in place'],['Irrigation','Flushing a wound with clean water'],['Impaled object','Stabilize, do not remove'],['Amputated part','Wrap in gauze, bag and keep cool'],['Signs of infection','Redness, swelling, warmth and pus']])]],
['Fractures and dislocations',[
'A **fracture** is a broken bone—**closed** (skin intact) or **open** (bone ends through the skin, risking infection). A **dislocation** is displacement of bones at a joint; a **sprain** injures a ligament; a **strain** injures a muscle or tendon. Signs include **deformity, swelling, bruising, pain, tenderness, inability to use the limb** and grating sounds. Because CERT members cannot tell these apart without X-rays, **treat any of them as a fracture**.',
'Splints can be **rigid** (boards, cardboard, rolled magazines), **soft** (pillows, blankets, a sling and swathe) or **anatomical** (securing an injured leg to the uninjured leg or an arm to the chest). Open fractures are covered with a sterile dressing before splinting; do not push bones back in. Check **pulse, movement and sensation (PMS)** before and after splinting.',
sort('Injury type',['Fracture','Dislocation','Sprain','Strain'],[['Broken bone',0],['Bones displaced at a joint',1],['Torn ligament',2],['Pulled muscle or tendon',3],['Bone end visible through the skin',0],['Shoulder popped out of its socket',1]])]],
['Splinting a closed fracture: Skill V',[
'Skill V is done by **both rescuers together** in **5 minutes** on one rating sheet. **1.** Don PPE. **2.** Size up. **3.** Identify a **conscious patient with a closed injury to an extremity**. **4.** Verbalize that you suspect a fracture, dislocation, sprain or strain and will **treat it as a fracture**. **5.** **Assess PMS** in the limb before splinting. **6.** Look for and identify **splinting materials** (from your kit or the immediate area). **7.** **Support the injured area above and below** the injury.',
'**8.** Remove restrictive clothing, shoes and jewelry if necessary. **9.** Apply the splint **in the position found**, without trying to realign bones or joints. **10.** Secure the splint with ties that hold it in place **without compromising circulation**. **11.** **Fill voids** with padding to stabilize the injury. **12.** **Immobilize the joints above and below** the injury. **13.** **Reassess PMS** and compare with the first check, verbalizing findings. **14.** Hand hygiene. **15–16.** Both rescuers communicate throughout.',
order('Skill V sequence',['PPE and size-up','Identify the closed extremity injury; treat as a fracture','Check PMS','Find splint materials','Support above and below the injury','Remove restrictive clothing and jewelry','Splint in the position found','Secure without cutting off circulation; fill voids','Immobilize the joints above and below','Recheck PMS and compare'])]],
['Sanitation and public health',[
'After disasters, illness from contaminated water and poor sanitation can cause more harm than the disaster itself. **Water** can be made safer by **boiling** it for **1 minute** (3 minutes at high elevations) or adding unscented household **bleach** as directed (about **8 drops of regular bleach per gallon** of clear water, waiting 30 minutes). Use designated **latrines** away from water sources, keep waste covered and wash hands.',
'Control flies and rodents, store food safely, separate sick people when possible and keep treatment areas clean. CERT members also watch their own health: rest, hydrate, eat and get vaccinations such as tetanus as recommended.',
"Food safety matters too: discard refrigerated food that has been above **40 °F** for more than about **2 hours** during a power outage, and throw away food touched by flood water. Generators must be used **outdoors**, far from windows and doors, to prevent **carbon monoxide poisoning**, which causes headache, dizziness and confusion.",
chk('How long should water be boiled to make it safe in most locations?',['1 minute at a rolling boil','10 seconds','30 minutes','It cannot be made safe'],'3 minutes at elevations above about 6,500 feet.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Splinting decision',{a:{text:'A conscious man has a swollen, deformed forearm with intact skin. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you assume?',choices:[['Treat it as a fracture','b','Correct. CERT treats all such injuries as fractures.',true],['It is only a bruise','b','Deformity and swelling suggest a fracture.',false]]},b:{text:'Before splinting, what do you check?',choices:[['Pulse, movement and sensation in the hand','c','Correct. Compare after splinting.',true],['Nothing; just splint quickly','c','Baseline PMS is required.',false]]},c:{text:'You splint the arm in the position found, immobilize the wrist and elbow, add a sling, recheck PMS and document the result.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Burns: superficial (red, dry, painful, swollen), partial thickness (blisters), full thickness (charred or leathery, may be painless); stop the burning, cool with water, cover loosely, keep warm, treat shock.','Irrigate and dress wounds, stabilize impaled objects, bag and cool amputated parts; treat fractures, dislocations, sprains and strains as fractures.','Skill V: PPE, size-up, PMS check, support above and below, remove restrictions, splint as found, secure without compromising circulation, fill voids, immobilize joints above and below, recheck PMS; boil water 1 minute.'],terms:[['Closed fracture','A broken bone with the skin intact.'],['Splint','A device that immobilizes an injured body part.'],['Partial-thickness burn','A burn into the dermis that causes blisters.']],mcqs:[q('Which burn classification is characterized by reddened, dry skin, pain and swelling?','Superficial','Partial thickness','Complete','Full thickness','HOSA sample question.'),q('Blisters indicate which burn depth?','Partial thickness','Superficial','Full thickness','None','Second degree.'),q('How should a burn be cooled?','With cool, clean water','With ice','With butter','With ointment','Then cover loosely.'),q('A suspected sprain in CERT is treated as:','A fracture','Nothing','A bruise','A burn','Cannot rule out a break.'),q('Splints are applied:','In the position found','After straightening the bone','Only to the wrist','Without padding','Do not realign.'),q('Before and after splinting, check:','Pulse, movement and sensation','Blood pressure only','Temperature only','Nothing','PMS.'),q('An impaled object should be:','Stabilized in place','Pulled out quickly','Pushed further in','Ignored','Prevents more bleeding.'),q('How long should water be boiled to make it safe at most elevations?','1 minute','10 seconds','1 hour','It should not be boiled','Rolling boil.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how a CERT team splints a closed fracture of the lower leg following HOSA Skill V, and explain why each step matters.',rubric:[['Start',['ppe','size-up','conscious','closed','treat as a fracture']],['Assessment and materials',['pms','pulse','movement','sensation','splinting materials','support above and below']],['Splinting',['remove','clothing','jewelry','position found','realign','secure','circulation','fill voids','joints above and below']],['Reassessment and teamwork',['reassess','compare','verbalize','hand rub','communicate','partner']]],model:'Wearing PPE, we size up the scene and identify a conscious patient with a closed injury to the lower leg. We say that it could be a fracture, dislocation, sprain or strain and that we will treat it as a fracture, because we cannot tell them apart without X-rays. We check pulse, movement and sensation in the foot to establish a baseline, then look for splinting materials such as boards, rolled blankets or cardboard. One rescuer supports the leg above and below the injury to prevent movement while the other removes restrictive clothing, shoes or jewelry if needed, because swelling could cut off circulation. We apply the splint in the position found without trying to straighten the bones, which could damage nerves and vessels, and secure it with ties firm enough to hold it but not so tight that they compromise circulation. We fill voids with padding so the leg cannot shift, and we immobilize the joints above and below the injury—the knee and ankle—so the broken bone cannot move. Then we reassess pulse, movement and sensation and compare with the first check, telling the judge if anything changed. We finish with hand rub and keep communicating with the patient and each other throughout.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_BURNS,MEDLINE_FRACTURE,CDC_WATER,IS317]};

export const K9={title:'Light search and rescue, lifts and carries (Skill IV)',hook:'In many disasters, most people rescued from damaged buildings are saved by neighbors and bystanders in the first hours—long before specialized teams arrive. But untrained rescuers are also hurt or killed when they enter unsafe buildings. Light search and rescue is 15% of the test, and Skill IV tests four ways to move victims to safety.',objectives:['Size up structures and classify light, moderate and heavy damage.','Conduct a systematic search and use search markings.','Use leveraging and cribbing and know rescue safety limits.','Perform lifts and carries following HOSA Skill IV.'],sections:[
['Search and rescue size-up',[
'The goals of light search and rescue are to **rescue the greatest number of people in the shortest time** while keeping rescuers safe. Size-up applies here: gather facts (time of day, type of building, weather, hazards), assess damage, consider probabilities (aftershocks, fire), assess your own situation and resources, set priorities, make a plan, act and evaluate.',
'Structural damage is classified as **light** (superficial damage, broken windows, fallen plaster, minor cracks—CERT may search), **moderate** (decorative work damaged, many cracks, building may have shifted on its foundation—CERT may search **with caution**, quickly removing easily accessible victims) and **heavy** (partial or total collapse, tilted, off its foundation, gas leaks or water—CERT should **not enter**; mark it and report it).',
sort('Damage level',['Light—search','Moderate—search with caution','Heavy—do not enter'],[['Broken windows and fallen plaster',0],['Building shifted on its foundation with many cracks',1],['Partial roof collapse',2],['Leaning walls and a gas odor',2],['Minor cracks in drywall',0],['Fallen decorative facade and large cracks',1]])]],
['Conducting a search',[
'Searches are **systematic**: work in **buddy teams**, call out (“If anyone can hear my voice, come here” or “Tap three times”), **stop and listen**, use a search pattern such as **right wall/left wall** (follow one wall through the building), **bottom-up or top-down** and **triangulation** (several rescuers listening from different points to locate a sound). Check under furniture, in closets and in other void spaces where people may be trapped.',
'**Search markings** communicate with other teams. At the entry point, draw a **single diagonal slash** when entering; complete an **X** when leaving. Quadrants of the X hold information: **left**—agency or group ID; **top**—date and time out; **right**—hazards found; **bottom**—number of live and dead victims. Mark rooms searched so others do not repeat the work.',
match('Search marking quadrants',[['Single slash','Team has entered'],['Completed X','Team has left'],['Left quadrant','Agency or group ID'],['Top quadrant','Date and time out'],['Right quadrant','Hazards'],['Bottom quadrant','Number of live and dead victims']])]],
['Rescue techniques and safety',[
'CERT rescues focus on **lightly trapped** victims. **Leveraging and cribbing** lift heavy objects safely: use a lever (such as a pry bar or board) and a fulcrum to raise an object a little at a time, and slide **cribbing** (wood blocks in a box pattern) underneath—“**lift an inch, crib an inch**”—so the load is always supported. Never put any part of your body under a raised load.',
'Before moving a victim, assess for injuries and spinal involvement. Move victims only if they are in danger or must be moved to reach others. Rescuers use proper body mechanics—keep the back straight, lift with the legs, keep the load close and avoid twisting—and know their own limits.',
order('Leveraging and cribbing',['Size up the load and the victim','Place the fulcrum and lever','Lift the object a little','Insert cribbing under it','Repeat: lift an inch, crib an inch','Remove the victim once the load is supported'])]],
['Lifts and carries: Skill IV steps 1–5',[
'Skill IV takes **8 minutes**; team members demonstrate some lifts on each other. **1.** Don PPE. **2.** Size up. **3.** Verbalize your **safety, capability and limitations** and decide whether and how to approach. **4. One-person pack-strap carry** (on your teammate): **a.** say, “I’m going to carry you to safety”; **b.** position yourself facing **away** from the patient; **c.** place the patient’s **arms over your shoulders** and grasp their hands in front of your chest; **d.** bend forward slightly to hoist the patient until their feet just clear the floor; **e.** carry them to safety and lower them gently, keeping your back straight and using your legs.',
'**5. Two-person carry** (on a small volunteer or manikin): **a.** say, “We are going to carry you to safety”; **b.** Rescuer 1 squats at the patient’s **head**, grasps from behind the midsection, reaches under the arms and grasps the patient’s **left wrist with the right hand and right wrist with the left hand**; **c.** Rescuer 2 squats **between the patient’s knees**, facing toward or away, and grasps the **outside of the legs at the knees**; **d.** both rise together, lifting with their legs; **e.** carry to the safe area and lower gently; **f.** communicate and coordinate throughout.',
order('Two-person carry',['Tell the patient you will carry them','Rescuer 1 at the head reaches under the arms and grasps opposite wrists','Rescuer 2 between the knees grasps outside the legs at the knees','Rise together lifting with the legs','Carry to the safe area','Lower gently while communicating'])]],
['Chair carry and blanket drag: Skill IV steps 6–10',[
'**6. Chair carry** (simulated only—do not actually lift): **a.** tell the patient, “We are going to carry you to safety”; **b.** Rescuer 1 crosses the patient’s arms in their lap, faces the **back** of the chair and grasps the **back uprights**; **c.** Rescuer 2 grasps the **two front legs** of the chair; **d.** both **tilt the chair back** and verbalize lifting together; **e.** verbalize carrying to the safe area and gently set the chair down; **f.** communicate throughout. The chair carry is useful on stairs or narrow paths.',
'**7. Blanket drag** (on your teammate): **a.** check that the path has **no debris** that could cause injury; **b.** say, “I’m going to wrap you in a blanket and move you to safety”; **c.** tell the judge you will use a **log roll** to position the patient on the blanket; **d.** wrap the patient securely; **e.** squat at the patient’s **head**, grasp the blanket behind the head with support; **f.** drag the patient clear of the hazard. **8.** Hand rub. **9–10.** Both rescuers communicate throughout.',
flip('Lift and carry key points',[['Pack-strap carry','Face away; patient’s arms over your shoulders'],['Two-person carry','Rescuer 1 at the head grasps wrists; Rescuer 2 holds knees'],['Chair carry','Simulated only; tilt the chair back'],['Blanket drag','Log roll onto blanket; pull from the head'],['Body mechanics','Straight back, lift with legs'],['Communication','Coordinate every movement']])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Search decision',{a:{text:'After an earthquake, a house has a partially collapsed roof and you hear a faint voice inside. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Do not enter; mark the location and report the trapped person to professional rescuers','b','Correct. Heavy damage is beyond CERT.',true],['Crawl in quickly to reach the person','b','You could become a victim.',false]]},b:{text:'Next door, a lightly damaged home has a man pinned under a fallen bookcase. What technique do you use?',choices:[['Leverage and crib—lift an inch, crib an inch—then remove him','c','Correct. Keep the load supported.',true],['Lift the bookcase by hand while one person reaches underneath','c','Never put body parts under an unsupported load.',false]]},c:{text:'You remove the man with a blanket drag, mark the house and report your findings.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Rescue the greatest number in the least time while staying safe; light damage—search; moderate—search with caution; heavy—do not enter.','Search systematically in buddy teams (call out, listen, right/left wall, triangulate); mark a slash on entry and an X on exit with ID, time, hazards and victims in the quadrants.','Lift an inch, crib an inch; Skill IV: pack-strap (face away, arms over shoulders), two-person carry (head and knees), chair carry (simulated, tilt back) and blanket drag (log roll, pull from the head) with good body mechanics and communication.'],terms:[['Cribbing','Wood blocks placed under a lifted object to support it.'],['Triangulation','Locating a victim by listening from several points.'],['Search marking','Symbols showing a team entered, left and what it found.']],mcqs:[q('Which building damage level means CERT should not enter?','Heavy','Light','Moderate','None','Partial or total collapse.'),q('What does a single slash at an entry point mean?','A team has entered','A team has left','No one is inside','The building is safe','Complete an X when leaving.'),q('In a search marking X, the bottom quadrant shows:','Number of live and dead victims','Agency ID','Hazards','Date and time','Victim count.'),q('The rule for cribbing is:','Lift an inch, crib an inch','Lift a foot, crib a foot','Lift and hold','Never crib','Keep the load supported.'),q('In the pack-strap carry, the rescuer faces:','Away from the patient','Toward the patient','Sideways','Up','Arms over the shoulders.'),q('Which lift is simulated only in Skill IV?','Chair carry','Pack-strap carry','Blanket drag','Two-person carry','Do not actually lift.'),q('In the blanket drag, the patient is placed on the blanket using a:','Log roll','Fireman’s carry','Chair lift','Pull by the arms','Protects the spine.'),q('In the two-person carry, Rescuer 2 grasps:','The outside of the legs at the knees','The head','The wrists','The belt','Rescuer 1 has the upper body.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how a CERT team decides whether to search a damaged building and describe two lifts or carries from HOSA Skill IV that the team could use to remove victims.',rubric:[['Size-up and damage',['size-up','light','moderate','heavy','do not enter','caution','report']],['Search technique',['buddy','call out','listen','pattern','triangulate','mark','slash','x']],['Lift or carry 1',['pack-strap','face away','arms over','shoulders','hoist','legs']],['Lift or carry 2',['two-person','head','wrists','knees','rise together','blanket drag','log roll','communicate']]],model:'The team first sizes up the building and its own resources. If the building has light damage, such as broken windows and minor cracks, the team may search it; with moderate damage, such as many cracks or a shift on its foundation, the team may search with caution, quickly removing easily reached victims; with heavy damage, such as a partial collapse, leaning walls or a gas leak, the team does not enter, marks the location and reports any trapped victims to professional rescuers. When searching, rescuers work in buddy teams, call out for anyone who can hear them, stop and listen, follow a systematic pattern such as right wall, triangulate on sounds and mark the entry with a slash, completing an X with ID, time, hazards and victim counts when they leave. To remove a conscious adult who can hold on, one rescuer can use the pack-strap carry: telling the patient what will happen, facing away, placing the patient’s arms over the shoulders and holding the hands at the chest, bending forward slightly to lift until the feet clear the floor and lowering them gently in the safe area using the legs. For a patient who cannot help, two rescuers can use the two-person carry: Rescuer 1 squats at the head, reaches under the arms and grasps the opposite wrists, while Rescuer 2 squats between the knees and grasps the outside of the legs at the knees; they rise together, carry the patient to safety and lower them gently, communicating throughout.'},sources:[RULES,IS317,READY_CERT]};

export const K10={title:'Disaster psychology and terrorism',hook:'Disasters injure minds as well as bodies. Survivors and rescuers alike may feel shock, fear, grief and exhaustion, and a calm, caring CERT member can make a lasting difference. Terrorist events add special dangers—secondary devices, chemicals or radiation. Disaster psychology and terrorism are each 5% of the test.',objectives:['Describe common emotional and physical reactions to disasters in survivors and rescuers.','Apply psychological first aid and protect rescuer well-being.','Recognize CBRNE terrorism and warning signs.','Describe CERT protective actions during a suspected terrorist event.'],sections:[
['Disaster reactions',[
'After a disaster, people often pass through phases: **impact** (shock and confusion), **inventory** (assessing damage and searching for loved ones), **rescue** (activity and cooperation) and **recovery** (rebuilding, which may bring frustration, grief and fatigue). Common reactions include **fear, anxiety, sadness, anger, guilt, numbness, trouble sleeping, headaches, stomach problems, difficulty concentrating** and irritability. These are **normal reactions to abnormal events**.',
'Some groups need extra attention: **children** (who may regress, cling or fear separation), **older adults**, **people with disabilities**, people with **prior trauma** and **first responders**. Signs that someone needs professional help include thoughts of self-harm, inability to function, substance misuse and symptoms lasting weeks.',
order('Disaster phases',['Impact','Inventory','Rescue','Recovery'])]],
['Psychological first aid',[
'**Psychological first aid (PFA)** helps survivors feel **safe, calm, connected, hopeful and able to help themselves**. CERT members: introduce themselves; provide comfort and basic needs (water, blankets, shelter); **listen** without forcing people to talk; give accurate, simple information; help people connect with family and resources; and encourage practical coping. Avoid saying “I know how you feel” or giving false reassurance.',
'CERT members also do **on-scene stabilization**: remove survivors from the sight of the scene when possible, give them simple tasks, keep families together and protect their privacy from onlookers and media. The **Disaster Distress Helpline** (call or text **1-800-985-5990**) offers 24/7 crisis counseling.',
sort('Helpful or unhelpful?',['Helpful PFA','Unhelpful'],[['Listening without pressuring someone to talk',0],['Saying “I know exactly how you feel”',1],['Helping someone contact family',0],['Giving accurate, simple information',0],['Promising everything will be fine',1],['Offering water and a blanket',0]])]],
['Rescuer self-care and team well-being',[
'Rescuers face **traumatic stress**—seeing injuries and death, long hours and difficult decisions such as triage. Protect team well-being by **rotating** tasks and taking breaks, eating, hydrating and sleeping, staying in contact with family, using the **buddy system** to watch for stress in each other and holding a **brief defusing** or check-in after a shift.',
'Warning signs of severe stress in rescuers include confusion, inability to make decisions, isolation, crying, hyperactivity, physical symptoms and substance use. Team leaders should relieve affected members and refer them for support. Seeking help is a sign of strength.',
"Leaders can reduce stress before it builds by giving clear assignments, sharing accurate information, limiting shift length, recognizing good work and making sure no one works alone. After the incident, a **debriefing** helps the team review what went well, what to improve and how everyone is coping.",
match('Self-care strategy',[['Rotation','Change tasks to reduce strain'],['Buddy system','Watch each other for stress'],['Defusing','Short check-in after a shift'],['Basic needs','Food, water and rest'],['Family contact','Reduces worry'],['Referral','Professional support when needed']])]],
['Terrorism and CBRNE',[
'**Terrorism** is the use of violence to intimidate or coerce for political or ideological goals. Weapons may be **CBRNE**: **C**hemical (nerve agents, toxic industrial chemicals), **B**iological (anthrax, toxins), **R**adiological (a “dirty bomb” spreading radioactive material), **N**uclear and **E**xplosive (the most common). Terrorists may also use cyberattacks, vehicles or firearms.',
'Warning signs of a chemical or biological event: **many people with similar symptoms** (difficulty breathing, eye irritation, vomiting, convulsions), **dead animals**, unusual **odors, mists or liquids**, and unexplained containers. Explosions may be followed by **secondary devices** targeting responders. Report suspicious activity: “**If You See Something, Say Something.**”',
match('CBRNE',[['C','Chemical agents such as nerve agents'],['B','Biological agents such as anthrax'],['R','Radiological dispersal device (dirty bomb)'],['N','Nuclear weapon'],['E','Explosives—the most common'],['Secondary device','Second bomb targeting responders']])]],
['CERT actions during a terrorist event',[
'CERT members do **not** respond into a suspected terrorist or hazmat scene. **Protect yourself**: leave the area, moving **upwind, uphill and upstream**; avoid touching anything; and keep others away. Report what you saw to authorities. If exposed to a chemical or radiological agent, **remove outer clothing** (which can remove most contamination), place it in a sealed bag, and wash with soap and water. For a radiation emergency, **get inside, stay inside and stay tuned**.',
'After an explosion, be alert for secondary devices, structural collapse, fire and crowds. Help direct people away from the scene to a safe area, give first aid only where it is safe and follow instructions from police and fire officials. Terrorism also causes strong psychological reactions, so PFA is especially important.',
"For a **suspicious package**—unexpected, oddly addressed, with stains, wires or odors—do not touch, shake or open it; leave the area, keep others away and call authorities. In an **active threat** situation, remember **Run, Hide, Fight**: escape if you can, hide if you cannot and fight only as a last resort.",
kase('Suspicious event',{a:{text:'At a crowded event, several people suddenly collapse coughing with watery eyes, and there is a strange smell. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Leave the area upwind and uphill, keep others away and report it','b','Correct. Suspect a chemical release.',true],['Run toward the victims to help','b','You could become a victim.',false]]},b:{text:'You may have been exposed. What should you do first?',choices:[['Remove outer clothing, bag it and wash with soap and water','c','Correct. Removing clothing removes most contamination.',true],['Keep your clothes on and wait','c','Decontaminate as soon as possible.',false]]},c:{text:'You follow instructions from responders and provide psychological first aid to others in the safe area.',end:true}})]],
["Long-term recovery and community resilience",["Recovery can take months or years. Survivors may face housing loss, financial stress, health problems and grief long after responders leave. Communities recover better when they have strong social connections, accurate information, access to services and opportunities for people to help each other. CERT members can support recovery by connecting neighbors with resources, helping at distribution centers and checking on vulnerable residents.","**Anniversary reactions**—renewed distress around the date of a disaster—are common. Schools, faith groups and community organizations can provide ongoing support. Building **resilience** before the next disaster—through preparedness, training, mental-health awareness and community relationships—closes the emergency management cycle.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Supports resilience?","buckets":["Supports recovery and resilience","Hinders recovery"],"items":[["Checking on isolated neighbors",0],["Spreading rumors on social media",1],["Sharing official information",0],["Ignoring signs of long-term distress",1],["Joining a community preparedness group",0],["Volunteering at a distribution center",0]]}]]
],takeaways:['Disaster phases: impact, inventory, rescue and recovery; fear, grief, anger, sleep problems and numbness are normal reactions to abnormal events; watch children, older adults and responders.','Psychological first aid: safety, calm, connection, accurate information and basic needs; avoid false reassurance; rescuers rotate, rest, use buddies and defuse; Disaster Distress Helpline 1-800-985-5990.','CBRNE (chemical, biological, radiological, nuclear, explosive); signs include many people with similar symptoms and dead animals; leave upwind, uphill and upstream, report, remove outer clothing and wash; beware secondary devices.'],terms:[['Psychological first aid','Supportive actions that help people feel safe, calm and connected after a disaster.'],['CBRNE','Chemical, biological, radiological, nuclear and explosive threats.'],['Secondary device','A second explosive set to harm responders.']],mcqs:[q('Which phase involves searching for loved ones and assessing damage?','Inventory','Impact','Recovery','Mitigation','After impact.'),q('Which is a helpful psychological first aid action?','Listening without forcing someone to talk','Promising everything will be fine','Telling people to calm down','Leaving them alone immediately','Supportive.'),q('What does the R in CBRNE stand for?','Radiological','Rescue','Respiratory','Response','Dirty bomb.'),q('Which is a sign of a possible chemical attack?','Many people with similar symptoms at once','One person with a sprained ankle','Rain','A traffic jam','Plus odd odors or dead animals.'),q('Where should you move during a suspected chemical release?','Upwind, uphill and upstream','Downwind','Toward the source','Underground','Avoid the plume.'),q('What removes most contamination after exposure?','Removing outer clothing','Drinking water','Waiting','Running','Then wash.'),q('Which is the most common type of terrorist weapon?','Explosives','Nuclear','Biological','Radiological','E in CBRNE.'),q('What helps protect rescuer well-being?','Rotating tasks and taking breaks','Working 36 hours straight','Avoiding teammates','Skipping meals','Self-care.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how CERT members support the emotional well-being of disaster survivors and their own team, and explain what they should do if they suspect a terrorist chemical release.',rubric:[['Survivor reactions',['normal','fear','grief','anger','sleep','children','older adults']],['Psychological first aid',['safe','calm','listen','basic needs','information','connect','family','avoid','helpline']],['Rescuer self-care',['rotate','breaks','rest','buddy','defusing','refer']],['Terrorism response',['similar symptoms','odor','leave','upwind','uphill','report','outer clothing','wash','secondary']]],model:'CERT members understand that fear, sadness, anger, guilt, numbness, sleep problems and trouble concentrating are normal reactions to an abnormal event, and they pay special attention to children, older adults, people with disabilities and anyone with prior trauma. Using psychological first aid, they introduce themselves, move survivors away from the sight of the scene, provide water, blankets and shelter, listen without pressuring people to talk, give accurate and simple information, help people reach family members and resources and avoid false promises like “everything will be fine.” They refer people with serious distress to professional help and share the Disaster Distress Helpline. For their own team, leaders rotate tasks, schedule breaks, make sure members eat, drink and rest, use the buddy system to watch for stress and hold a brief defusing after shifts, relieving and referring members who show severe stress. If CERT members suspect a terrorist chemical release—for example, many people suddenly coughing with watery eyes, an unusual odor or dead animals—they do not enter the scene. They leave, moving upwind, uphill and upstream, keep others away and report what they saw to authorities, watching for secondary devices. If they may have been exposed, they remove their outer clothing, seal it in a bag and wash with soap and water, then follow responders’ instructions.'},sources:[RULES,NCTSN_PFA,SAMHSA_DDH,READY_CHEM,READY_RAD,DHS_SEE]};
